CI on this PR went red on two `test_cfg.py::TestUnaccountedNames` cases
that are green locally. The cause was not the change under review: the
predicate they exercise reads statements that `populate_def_use_for_cfg`
fills, that pass NO-OPS when no def/use extractor is registered for the
language, and the registry is populated by import side effect — so with
no Go extractor registered every statement has empty `defines`,
`unaccounted_names` answers EMPTY for any input, and the tests pass or
fail by whether some other test in the worker had imported
`go_def_use`. A different slice gives a different xdist distribution,
which is all it took.

`ensure_def_use_extractors_registered` already guards the neighbouring
hazard (an import is a no-op once the module is in `sys.modules`, so it
cannot re-register after a clear) by comparing the live registry against
a LEARNED expected set. The learned set could itself be clipped: if the
first call in a process found a partial registry — non-empty, so the
reload branch did not fire — it recorded that as health, and every later
subset check passed against the clipped baseline. Measured: the helper
returning True with javascript/python/typescript registered and `go`
missing, permanently. The first call now reloads unconditionally and
learns from the state it produced itself.

The tests stop inheriting their configuration: an autouse fixture
establishes the production one and ASSERTS the Go extractor is present,
because a class whose predicate answers empty for every input would pass
by reporting nothing. INV-lupav clause L4 is unaffected — it reproduces
exactly as written once the extractor is registered.

Closes INV-zunik

